From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: ntdll: collapse duplicate separators after a UNC share

RtlGetFullPathName_U treats everything up to the end of \\server\share and
*all* the separators after it as the UNC root, which it never collapses:
\\server\share\\file stayed as it was, and then failed to open with
ERROR_INVALID_NAME. Windows collapses duplicate separators everywhere else
in a path (c:///test is c:\test), and cmd builds exactly this name for any
script at the root of a share: it appends "\" and the file name to the
directory, which already ends in "\". So "cmd /c \\DOMAIN\NETLOGON\logon.bat"
-- every domain logon script -- failed with "Can't recognize ... as an
internal or external command".

The UNC root now ends at the first separator after the share name, and any
more are collapsed with the rest of the path. ntdll:path and kernel32:path
conformance unchanged (0 failures).

--- a/dlls/ntdll/path.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/path.c
@@ -503,7 +503,10 @@
     while (*ptr && !IS_SEPARATOR(*ptr)) ptr++;  /* share name */
     while (IS_SEPARATOR(*ptr)) ptr++;
     while (*ptr && !IS_SEPARATOR(*ptr)) ptr++;  /* dir name */
-    while (IS_SEPARATOR(*ptr)) ptr++;
+    /* one separator ends the prefix; any more are collapsed with the rest of
+     * the path (\\server\share\\file is \\server\share\file, as c:\\file
+     * is c:\file) */
+    if (IS_SEPARATOR(*ptr)) ptr++;
     return ptr;
 }
 
